# A scanner's answer is a two-field record that travels in three registers:
# both tags in the first word, the value's payload in the second, the
# position in the third. A consumer whose arm destructures the record reads
# the words directly and no record is ever built. An arm that names the
# whole value, like `r@(parsed p v)`, wants the record itself, so a slot
# with such an arm is passed boxed for every arm, and the scanner builds a
# record per answer.
#
# `e@(err _)` is not that arm. It matches only an err a rescue took out of
# its box (#2087), and a failure passes it, so at a slot the two words carry
# it never fires and no record is wanted. Under the 2026-09-15 ruling every
# consumer of a fallible scanner wrote an arm like this one, and until
# 2026-09-16 the arm turned the register convention off: the json decoder
# built 253,968,000 bytes of records it had built none of the day before.
# Programs written then still carry such arms.
#
# The walk stops at the first position, so the scanner's failure is never
# reached and the loop hands back a number all the way up. `sh_rec` is the
# pin: 0 with the arm read as the err case it is, 64,000 with it read as an
# ordinary as-pattern, a record for each of the thousand scans.
type parsed
  pos
  val

fn scan 0
  err "end of input"

fn scan n
  parsed n (n * 2)

fn step (parsed 1 v)
  v

fn step (parsed p v)
  v + total (p - 1)

fn step e@(err _)
  length e.reason

fn total n
  step (scan n)

pub play = print "total {total 1000}"
